PROTOTYPE ONLY (ADR 0027 proposal, the one-day look of "new windows" on the Linux host,
docs/measurements/2026-11-20-new-windows-ubuntu.md). Never in a build that a trial, the gate or a
verdict uses; scripts/build-compositor.sh does not know it (it is in a folder of its own, and it
is not a trial-*.patch).

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of the four measured patches in scripts/patches
(render-on-commit, cursor-messages, windows, dialogs, in that order). The lines of context are
from that project and from those patches.

What it adds, and nothing more, to answer one question (does the first window's picture come
later while a second window is rendered and encoded at the same time?):

- The element `waylanddisplaysrc` takes `output` (0 unless set). An element with `output=1`
  runs no compositor: it attaches to the one that an element with `output=0` runs in the same
  process (found through a process-wide slot; it waits up to 10 s for it) and streams that
  compositor's second output. Its caps, its CUDA buffer pool and its frames go to the
  compositor as `VideoInfoFor(1, ..)`, `UpdateCUDABufferPoolFor(1, ..)` and
  `BufferFor(1, ..)`. With `output=0` nothing changes.
- The compositor (wayland-display-core/src/comp/second.rs): a second `wl_output`
  ("HEADLESS-2") mapped far to the right of the first in the space, so the first output's
  picture never holds anything of it; its own damage tracker and buffer (CUDA, DMABuf or
  memory, as the first); rendered on the beat of its own frame rate on the same thread and
  with the same GL context as the first output, without a cursor; its window is told to draw
  after each of its frames.
- The first toplevel without a parent that is configured while the first output already shows
  a window goes to the second output, fullscreen at its size, and does not take the keyboard.
  What it commits does not make the first output render or tell the first output's clients
  anything. When it goes, the next such window takes its place.

Not in it: pointer and keyboard for the second window, popups and dialogs on it, resizing,
more than two outputs, a second output that comes and goes while the compositor runs. No
tests of its own: the compositor's tests are run against it unchanged.

